---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import LocationPanel from '../components/LocationPanel.astro';
import PageHero from '../components/PageHero.astro';
import RelatedTools from '../components/RelatedTools.astro';
---

<Layout
	title="GPS Coordinates Online — Latitude, Longitude & DMS"
	description="Find my GPS coordinates online: latitude, longitude, altitude, and accuracy in decimal and DMS format. Copy, share, or open in Google Maps. No install, no tracking."
	path="/location/"
	keywords="gps coordinates online, find my location, latitude longitude, dms coordinates, my gps location"
>
	<PageHero
		eyebrow="gps · location"
		title="Know exactly where you are."
		description="Live GPS coordinates, altitude, and accuracy straight from your device. Everything is computed locally — your location never leaves your browser."
	>
		<LocationPanel />
	</PageHero>

	<section class="border-t border-hairline bg-canvas">
		<div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 py-20 sm:px-6 md:py-24">
			<p class="eyebrow">the gps location tool</p>
			<h2 class="mt-3 text-display-lg text-ink">Get GPS coordinates online — instantly</h2>

			<p class="mt-6 text-body-lg text-body">
				Need to <strong class="text-ink font-medium">find my GPS coordinates online</strong>?
				This tool reads your device's location in real time and shows your current latitude and
				longitude in both decimal degrees and degrees-minutes-seconds (DMS) format, along with
				your altitude and estimated accuracy. Grant permission once, and the page continuously
				tracks your position as you move — useful for hiking, field work, geocaching, or simply
				sharing exactly where you are with someone.
			</p>
			<p class="mt-4 leading-7">
				Everything is processed locally in your browser. Your location is never sent to a server,
				stored, or shared — this GPS coordinates online tool is private by design, and the
				reading disappears the moment you close the page.
			</p>

			<h3 class="mt-10 text-display-sm text-ink">Latitude, longitude, and DMS explained</h3>
			<p class="mt-3 leading-7">
				<strong class="text-ink font-medium">Latitude</strong> measures how far north or south of
				the equator you are (0° at the equator, 90° at the poles), while
				<strong class="text-ink font-medium">longitude</strong> measures how far east or west of
				the Prime Meridian you are. Together they form a grid that pinpoints any place on Earth
				to within meters. Most mapping apps use decimal degrees (for example 48.8584, 2.2945 for
				Paris), but many forms and navigation manuals expect DMS (48° 51' 29.6" N, 2° 17' 40.2" E).
				This tool shows <strong class="text-ink font-medium">my location</strong> in both formats
				so you can copy whichever one you need — no converting by hand.
			</p>
			<p class="mt-4 leading-7">
				Decimal degrees and DMS are the same position, just written differently. One degree of
				latitude is about 111 kilometres; one minute is about 1.85 kilometres; one second is
				about 30 metres. Understanding the units helps you judge how precise a coordinate really
				is — and why accuracy matters when you are navigating in the field.
			</p>

			<h3 class="mt-10 text-display-sm text-ink">Where can you use live GPS coordinates?</h3>
			<div class="mt-4 space-y-3">
				<p class="leading-7">
					<strong class="text-ink font-medium">Outdoor navigation.</strong> When you are on a
					trail, at a campsite, or exploring a new city, capture your coordinates so you can
					share them or navigate back. It is the fastest way to answer "where am I right now?"
				</p>
				<p class="leading-7">
					<strong class="text-ink font-medium">Photography and planning.</strong> Geo-tag a
					location for a shoot, record where you stood for a photo, or check the altitude of a
					viewpoint before you plan a route.
				</p>
				<p class="leading-7">
					<strong class="text-ink font-medium">Work and study.</strong> Surveyors, farmers, and
					field researchers use precise coordinates for marking plots and points. Students use
					them for geography projects and to cross-check map work.
				</p>
				<p class="leading-7">
					<strong class="text-ink font-medium">Other tools on this site.</strong> Your
					coordinates feed directly into the Qibla finder, bearing calculator, and sun position
					tool, so you can work with your exact position everywhere.
				</p>
			</div>

			<h3 class="mt-10 text-display-sm text-ink">How accurate is the GPS location?</h3>
			<p class="mt-3 leading-7">
				Modern phones combine satellite GPS, Wi-Fi, and cell-tower data. Outdoors with a clear sky
				you can usually get accuracy within 3–10 metres; indoors the signal weakens and accuracy
				drops. The tool shows your estimated accuracy in metres so you know how much to trust the
				reading. For the best fix, stand still for a few seconds outdoors, away from tall
				buildings and dense tree cover.
			</p>
			<p class="mt-4 leading-7">
				Because this is a GPS coordinates online tool that never uploads anything, you can use it
				with confidence anywhere — a private way to find my location that also gives you the
				precision you need.
			</p>
		<h3 class="mt-10 text-display-sm text-ink">Share, copy, and open your coordinates</h3>
			<p class="mt-3 leading-7">
				The tool is built for real use, not just display. Copy your coordinates in the format you
				need, share them with a friend, or open your position directly in Google Maps for a wider
				view of the area. Combined with the live accuracy reading, you can confidently record a
				spot now and return to it later — a handy waypoint for hikes, meetings, or deliveries.
			</p>
			<p class="mt-4 leading-7">
				This GPS coordinates online tool works alongside the rest of the site: your current
				position becomes the starting point for the
				<a href="/bearing/" class="text-link underline decoration-link/30 hover:decoration-link">bearing calculator</a>,
				the reference for the
				<a href="/qibla/" class="text-link underline decoration-link/30 hover:decoration-link">Qibla finder</a>,
				and the location for the
				<a href="/sun/" class="text-link underline decoration-link/30 hover:decoration-link">sun position</a>
				and
				<a href="/" class="text-link underline decoration-link/30 hover:decoration-link">live compass</a>.
				One accurate fix, and every direction tool on this site uses it.
			</p>

			<h3 class="mt-10 text-display-sm text-ink">Why compute coordinates locally?</h3>
			<p class="mt-3 leading-7">
				Many "find my location" sites send your coordinates to a server the moment you load them.
				This page does the opposite: the GPS fix comes straight from your browser's geolocation
				API and is never transmitted anywhere. That means no tracking, no logs, and no risk of
				your position being stored against your wishes — a genuinely private way to find my GPS
				coordinates online. It is fast, free, and works on any modern phone or laptop with a
				browser.
			</p>
		</div>
	</section>

	<RelatedTools current="/location/" />
</Layout>